Continuous Cognitive Infrastructure

A Dynamical Systems Framework for Intelligence Without Agents

Author: Ouadi Maakoul
With generative assistance from ChatGPT, DeepSeek, Kimi, and Gemini

---

Abstract

We present a rigorous mathematical theory of intelligence as a continuous geometric process, independent of any notion of agent, planner, or symbolic system. The central object is a cognitive manifold equipped with a complete Riemannian metric and a real‑analytic cognitive potential that quantifies the coherence of a global cognitive state. The evolution of cognition is the gradient flow of this potential. Under natural analyticity and properness conditions we prove global existence, uniqueness, and convergence of every bounded trajectory to a critical point—generically a local minimum—using the Kurdyka–Łojasiewicz inequality. The theory is extended to distributed reasoning via a soft sheaf construction that weakly enforces consistency among local modules, and to the detection of paradigm shifts through Morse–theoretic transitions and persistent homology. Discrete approximations are obtained via the JKO minimizing movement scheme, with rigorous convergence guarantees. We also analyse metric approximations and controlled perturbations. The framework is entirely self‑contained: every theorem is deduced from the axioms, and empirical content is clearly separated. A concrete example—belief fusion on a probability simplex—demonstrates the theory’s applicability.

