Chapter 2: Mathematical Preliminaries

We assume familiarity with smooth manifold theory, Riemannian geometry, and functional analysis. This chapter fixes notation and recalls the essential theorems.

2.1 Manifolds and Riemannian Metrics

Let \mathcal{M} be a smooth, connected, paracompact manifold of dimension n<\infty. A Riemannian metric g assigns an inner product g_x: T_x\mathcal{M}\times T_x\mathcal{M}\to\mathbb{R}. The gradient of f\in C^1(\mathcal{M}) with respect to g is the unique vector field \nabla_g f satisfying g(\nabla_g f, v) = df(v) for all v\in T_x\mathcal{M}. The distance d_g(x,y) is the infimum of lengths of piecewise smooth curves joining x and y; it makes (\mathcal{M}, d_g) a metric space. Completeness of this metric space is equivalent, by Hopf–Rinow, to geodesic completeness and to closed bounded sets being compact.

2.2 The Fisher Information Metric (Prototype)

When cognitive states are probability distributions p(x|\theta) parameterised by \theta, the Fisher information matrix

g_{ij}(\theta) = \mathbb{E}_{p}\!\left[-\frac{\partial^2}{\partial\theta^i\partial\theta^j}\log p\right]
\]  

defines a Riemannian metric. This metric is unique in being Markov‑invariant (Čencov’s theorem). In CCI we do not require that \mathcal{M} arises from a statistical model; we assume only that a complete Riemannian metric is given.

2.3 Gradient Flows and Lyapunov Stability

Consider \dot{x} = V(x) with V\in C^1. For a gradient flow V=-\nabla_g\Phi, the function \Phi itself is a Lyapunov function: \frac{d}{dt}\Phi(x(t)) = -\|\nabla_g\Phi(x(t))\|_g^2 \le 0. A point x^* is a critical point if \nabla_g\Phi(x^*)=0, and a strict local minimum if the Hessian \operatorname{Hess}_g\Phi(x^*) (in normal coordinates) is positive definite.

2.4 The Kurdyka–Łojasiewicz (KL) Inequality

A differentiable function \Phi satisfies the KL inequality at a critical point x^* if there exist a neighbourhood U of x^*, constants c>0,\;\theta\in[0,1), and a continuous concave function \varphi:[0,\eta)\to\mathbb{R}_+ with \varphi(0)=0, \varphi\in C^1(0,\eta), such that for all x\in U with 0<\Phi(x)-\Phi(x^*)<\eta,

\varphi'(\Phi(x)-\Phi(x^*))\,\|\nabla_g\Phi(x)\|_g \ge 1.
\]  

The prototypical form is \varphi(s)=c\,s^{1-\theta}. Every real‑analytic function satisfies the KL inequality at each critical point (Łojasiewicz, 1963). If the critical point is isolated, one can always choose \theta\in(0,1/2]. This inequality is the main tool for proving convergence of gradient trajectories without convexity.

2.5 Sheaf Theory (Reminder)

A sheaf \mathcal{F} on a topological space X assigns to each open U\subset X a set \mathcal{F}(U) and restriction maps \rho_{UV}:\mathcal{F}(U)\to\mathcal{F}(V) for V\subset U, satisfying the gluing condition: if \{U_i\} covers U and sections s_i\in\mathcal{F}(U_i) agree on overlaps, there is a unique global section s\in\mathcal{F}(U) restricting to each s_i.

2.6 Persistent Homology and Morse Theory

Given a filtration \mathcal{M}_\epsilon = \{x\in\mathcal{M} : \Phi(x)\le\epsilon\}, the persistent homology PH_k(\epsilon) tracks the births and deaths of k-dimensional holes as \epsilon increases. The stability theorem (Cohen-Steiner et al., 2007) ensures that small perturbations of \Phi result in small changes in the persistence diagram.
Morse theory states that for a Morse function \Phi, the topology of the sublevel set \mathcal{M}_\epsilon changes only when \epsilon crosses a critical value; at a critical point of index k, a k-cell is attached.

2.7 The JKO Minimizing Movement Scheme

For a complete metric space (X,d) and function \Phi:X\to\mathbb{R}\cup\{+\infty\}, the JKO scheme (Jordan, Kinderlehrer, Otto; Ambrosio, Gigli, Savaré) generates discrete approximations of the gradient flow. Given a time step \tau>0, define recursively

x_{k+1} \in \arg\min_{x\in X} \left( \Phi(x) + \frac{1}{2\tau}d(x,x_k)^2 \right).
\]  

Under mild assumptions (properness, lower semicontinuity), the piecewise‑constant interpolant converges to the continuous gradient flow as \tau\to0.

