Chapter 3: Axioms of Continuous Cognitive Infrastructure

We state the fundamental objects and their governing laws. Every statement in this chapter is a definition; later theorems deduce consequences.

Axiom 1 (Cognitive Space).
There exists a smooth, connected, paracompact manifold \mathcal{M} of finite dimension, called the cognitive space. A point x\in\mathcal{M} represents a complete cognitive state of the system.

Axiom 2 (Semantic Metric).
\mathcal{M} is equipped with a complete Riemannian metric g. The geodesic distance d_g(x,y) measures the minimal informational deformation to transform one state into another.

Axiom 3 (Cognitive Potential).
There exists a function \Phi:\mathcal{M}\to\mathbb{R} that is smooth, real‑analytic, proper (preimages of compact sets are compact), and bounded below. Lower values of \Phi correspond to more coherent, less contradictory configurations.

Axiom 4 (Dynamic Principle).
The evolution of the cognitive state is the gradient flow

\frac{dx}{dt} = -\nabla_g \Phi(x), \qquad x(0)=x_0.
\]  

The flow is autonomous and deterministic; no external planner intervenes.

Remark. These four axioms form the core CCI. The next chapters derive the resulting dynamics. Distributed and compositional reasoning will be treated as a design pattern built on this core, not as an additional axiom.

