Chapter 4: Existence and Uniqueness of Cognitive Trajectories

Theorem 4.1 (Global Existence and Uniqueness).
Under Axioms 1–4, for any x_0\in\mathcal{M} the initial value problem

\dot{x}(t) = -\nabla_g \Phi(x(t)), \quad x(0)=x_0
\]  

has a unique solution x\in C^1([0,\infty),\mathcal{M}).

Proof. The vector field V=-\nabla_g\Phi is C^1 because \Phi\in C^2. By the Picard–Lindelöf theorem on manifolds, a unique local solution exists on [0,T_{\max}). Consider the Lyapunov function \Phi(x(t)):

\frac{d}{dt}\Phi(x(t)) = d\Phi(\dot{x}) = -\|\nabla_g\Phi(x)\|_g^2 \le 0.
\]  

Thus \Phi(x(t)) is non‑increasing, so the trajectory remains in the compact sublevel set \{y : \Phi(y) \le \Phi(x_0)\} (properness ensures compactness). A standard continuation argument shows that if T_{\max}<\infty the trajectory would have to escape every compact set, contradicting the invariance. Hence T_{\max}=\infty. Uniqueness follows from local Lipschitz continuity of V. ∎

