Chapter 5: Stability of Cognitive Attractors

Definition 5.1 (Cognitive Attractor).
A point x^*\in\mathcal{M} is a cognitive attractor if \nabla_g\Phi(x^*)=0 and the Hessian \operatorname{Hess}_g\Phi(x^*) is positive definite (strict local minimum).

Theorem 5.2 (Local Asymptotic Stability).
Let x^* be a cognitive attractor. Then x^* is asymptotically stable. Moreover, there exists a neighbourhood U and constants C,\lambda>0 such that for all x_0\in U,

d_g(x(t),x^*) \le C e^{-\lambda t}.
\]  

Proof. Work in normal coordinates centred at x^* with g_{ij}(0)=\delta_{ij} and \Gamma_{jk}^i(0)=0. Then

\Phi(y) = \Phi(0) + \tfrac12 y^T H y + o(\|y\|^2),
\]  

with H symmetric positive definite. The linearised flow is \dot{y} = -Hy, which is exponentially stable. The nonlinear flow inherits this by the stable manifold theorem, with Lyapunov function V(y)=\Phi(y)-\Phi(0) satisfying V\ge c_1\|y\|^2 and \dot{V}\le -c_2\|y\|^2 locally. Since in normal coordinates \|y\| is equivalent to d_g(x,x^*), exponential convergence in distance follows. ∎

