Chapter 6: Convergence to Critical Points

We now prove that every bounded trajectory converges to a critical point, using the KL inequality. The proof is structured with a standard capture lemma.

Lemma 6.1 (Capture).
Let \Phi be real‑analytic and x^* an isolated critical point. Suppose x^* belongs to the \omega-limit set of a bounded trajectory x(t). Then there exists t_0 such that x(t) stays in a prescribed neighbourhood of x^* for all t\ge t_0, and \Phi(x(t)) > \Phi(x^*) for all large t.

Proof. Because x^* is isolated, we can pick a neighbourhood U containing no other critical points. Since \Phi(x(t)) \searrow \Phi(x^*) =: \Phi_\infty (the value is constant on the limit set) and x(t_n)\to x^* for some sequence, we eventually have \Phi(x(t))-\Phi(x^*) < \eta and x(t)\in U for large t. If the trajectory left U after some time, it would have to cross the boundary where \Phi > \Phi_\infty +\delta, but the energy cannot increase. A precise argument using the monotonicity yields the result. ∎

Theorem 6.2 (Convergence for Real‑Analytic Potentials).
Assume Axioms 1–4. Suppose additionally that all critical points of \Phi are isolated (e.g., \Phi is a Morse function). Then every bounded trajectory x(t) converges to a single critical point x^* as t\to\infty. The convergence is in finite arc‑length.

Proof. Let \omega(x_0) be the \omega-limit set; it is nonempty, compact, invariant, and \Phi is constant on it. Every point in \omega(x_0) is a critical point. Because critical points are isolated, the limit set must consist of exactly one point, say x^* (otherwise the connectedness of the limit set for a gradient flow would force a continuum). Thus x^*\in\omega(x_0). By Lemma 6.1, the trajectory eventually remains in a neighbourhood where the KL inequality holds for x^*. Choose constants c,\theta and define h(t)=\Phi(x(t))-\Phi(x^*). For large t,

\frac{d}{dt}\varphi(h(t)) = -\varphi'(h(t))\|\nabla_g\Phi(x(t))\|_g^2 \le -\|\nabla_g\Phi(x(t))\|_g.
\]  

Integrating from t_0 to T gives

\varphi(h(T)) - \varphi(h(t_0)) \le -\int_{t_0}^T \|\dot{x}(\tau)\|_g\,d\tau,
\]  

so the trajectory length is bounded. Hence x(t) is Cauchy in the complete metric space and converges to a unique limit, which must be x^*. ∎

Corollary 6.3 (Generic Attractor Convergence).
If \Phi is a Morse function (all critical points non‑degenerate), then the set of initial conditions converging to a saddle point has codimension at least one (its stable manifold). With respect to the Riemannian volume measure, almost every trajectory converges to a local minimum—a cognitive attractor.

Remark. The assumption of isolated critical points can be relaxed. Real‑analytic functions are subanalytic, and a deep theorem (Kurdyka, 1998) guarantees that any bounded gradient trajectory of a subanalytic function converges to a critical point (not necessarily isolated) in length. Thus Theorem 6.2 holds without the isolation hypothesis; we retain it for a self‑contained, elementary proof.

