Chapter 7: Distributed Cognition via Soft Sheaves

The core CCI captures the evolution of a single cognitive manifold. Real‑world cognition often involves multiple distributed modules (e.g., different experts, data sources) that must reconcile their local views. We now show how to embed distributed reasoning as a construction within the CCI framework, without adding new axioms.

7.1 Local Modules and Consistency Energy

Suppose we have m cognitive modules. Each module i has its own state manifold \mathcal{M}_i with its own metric and local potential \Phi_i. The global state space is the product manifold \mathcal{M} = \mathcal{M}_1 \times \cdots \times \mathcal{M}_m, equipped with the product Riemannian metric g = g_1 \oplus \cdots \oplus g_m. Local potentials define a separable part \Phi_{\text{loc}}(x) = \sum_i \Phi_i(x_i).

To encourage consistency, we assume that for every pair of overlapping modules i,j there is a divergence measure D_{ij}: \mathcal{M}_i \times \mathcal{M}_j \to [0,\infty) quantifying the disagreement when their states are “compared” on their overlap. This could be a Kullback–Leibler divergence if the states are probability distributions, or a distance squared. The global cognitive potential is then

\Phi(x) = \Phi_{\text{loc}}(x) + \lambda \sum_{i,j} D_{ij}(x_i, x_j),

where \lambda>0 controls the strength of the consistency penalty. This potential is smooth and real‑analytic if the D_{ij} are. Properness must be checked; often it holds if each \Phi_i is proper and D_{ij} are coercive.

7.2 Soft Sheaf Interpretation

The collection of local manifolds and divergence measures can be viewed as a soft sheaf. The “gluing condition” is not exact equality on overlaps, but instead the minimisation of the discrepancy D_{ij}. The gradient flow on \mathcal{M} with potential \Phi simultaneously reduces each local incoherence and the inter‑module divergence. The limit points (minima of \Phi) are optimal global sections of this soft sheaf.

Theorem 7.1 (Distributed Gradient Flow).
Under the above construction, the global CCI dynamics \dot{x} = -\nabla_g \Phi(x) drives the modules toward a common attractor that balances local fidelity and global consistency. If \Phi has a unique global minimum, the flow converges to the best soft global section.

Proof. This is a direct application of Theorems 4.1 and 6.2 to the product manifold and the composed potential. The potential \Phi inherits real‑analyticity and properness from its components under mild conditions; the gradient flow exists globally and converges. ∎

Example (Belief Fusion).
Each module i maintains a belief q_i on a shared outcome space; \mathcal{M}_i is the probability simplex. Let D_{ij}(q_i,q_j) = D_{KL}(q_i\| q_j). Then the global potential is \sum_i w_i D_{KL}(q_i\| p_{i}^{\text{prior}}) + \lambda \sum_{i,j} D_{KL}(q_i\| q_j). The gradient flow converges to the consensus distribution that minimises the weighted divergence. (See Chapter 11 for a fully worked example.)

