Chapter 8: Discovery as Topological Phase Transition

8.1 Sublevel Filtration and Persistent Homology

Define the sublevel sets \mathcal{M}_\epsilon = \{x\in\mathcal{M} : \Phi(x) \le \epsilon\}. As the cognitive state flows downhill, the effective “energy” \epsilon decreases. Persistent homology captures topological features that persist over a range of \epsilon.

Definition 8.1 (Cognitive Filtration).
The family \{\mathcal{M}_\epsilon\}_{\epsilon \ge \inf\Phi} is a filtration. The k-th persistent homology PH_k(\epsilon) records births and deaths of k-dimensional holes.

Theorem 8.2 (Stability of Cognitive Topology, Cohen-Steiner et al.).
If \|\Phi - \Psi\|_\infty \le \delta, then the bottleneck distance between the persistence diagrams is \le \delta.

8.2 Morse Theory and Cognitive Phase Transitions

Assume \Phi is a Morse function (all critical points non‑degenerate). As the trajectory passes through a critical point of index k, the sublevel set homology changes by attaching a k-cell. The moment the flow crosses a critical value from above, a “paradigm shift” occurs: a new conceptual cluster (component), a loop (circular reasoning), or a higher‑dimensional void appears or disappears.

Theorem 8.3 (Morse Transitions).
Let x(t) be a gradient trajectory of a Morse function \Phi. At time t when \Phi(x(t)) crosses a critical value c, the topology of the set of states with energy \le \Phi(x(t)) changes exactly by the Morse index of the critical point. In particular, a transition from a disconnected sublevel set to a connected one (death of a 0‑dimensional feature) signals the merging of previously separate cognitive domains—a paradigmatic “insight”.

This observation provides a rigorous link between the dynamics of a single cognitive system and the qualitative restructuring of its coherent states. Features with large persistence correspond to robust conceptual structures.

