Chapter 9: Discrete Approximation and Metric Perturbations

9.1 The JKO Minimizing Movement Scheme

Theorem 9.1 (JKO Convergence on Manifolds).
Under Axioms 1–4, the JKO sequence defined by

x_{k+1} \in \arg\min_{x\in\mathcal{M}} \left( \Phi(x) + \frac{1}{2\tau} d_g(x,x_k)^2 \right), \quad x_0\in\mathcal{M},

is well‑defined (minimisers exist) and, as \tau\to0, the piecewise‑constant interpolant converges uniformly on compact time intervals to the unique gradient flow solution, in the sense of d_g.

Proof sketch. Properness guarantees compact sublevel sets, so the minimisation problem has a solution. The remainder is a direct adaptation of the theory of minimizing movements in complete metric spaces (Ambrosio, Gigli, Savaré, 2008, Theorem 4.0.4). The scheme is unconditionally stable: \Phi(x_{k+1})\le\Phi(x_k). ∎

9.2 Approximating the Metric

In practice the exact Riemannian metric may be replaced by an approximation \hat{g} (e.g., a Kronecker‑factored Fisher metric). The resulting dynamics are \dot{\hat{x}} = -\nabla_{\hat{g}} \Phi(\hat{x}).

Theorem 9.2 (Perturbation Bound).
Suppose \| \hat{g}_x^{-1} - g_x^{-1} \|_{\text{op}} \le \varepsilon on a compact set K that contains the trajectories. Then for initial conditions in K, the true flow x(t) and approximate flow \hat{x}(t) satisfy

d_g(x(T),\hat{x}(T)) \le C_T \varepsilon,

where C_T depends on the Lipschitz constant of d\Phi and the geometry of K.

Proof. The difference of the vector fields is bounded by \varepsilon \|d\Phi\|. A Gronwall argument on the metric deviation gives the estimate. ∎

Thus, metric approximations cause only controlled, bounded deviations, and the convergence to attractors is structurally preserved.

