Chapter 10: Continuous Intelligence Defined

We now extract the mathematical essence of intelligence within the CCI framework. The definition is emergent—it is a property of the whole cognitive infrastructure, not of any single module.

Definition 10.1 (Continuous Intelligence).
A pair (\mathcal{M},\Phi) satisfying Axioms 1–4 is said to exhibit continuous intelligence if the following properties hold:

1. Autonomous Coherence: Every bounded trajectory converges to a cognitive attractor (local minimum) of \Phi.
2. Soft Sheaf Compositionality: When the potential is constructed from local modules with a consistency penalty as in Chapter 7, the flow finds an optimal global section, reconciling local views without a central coordinator.
3. Morse Discoverability: \Phi is a Morse function, and the flow may undergo Morse–theoretic topological transitions, signalling paradigm shifts; these transitions are stable under small perturbations of \Phi.

A system that does not satisfy these properties—e.g., one that exhibits perpetual oscillations, requires an external planner to resolve inconsistencies, or lacks Morse structure—is not continuously intelligent by this definition.

Theorem 10.2 (Intelligence Without Agents).
If (\mathcal{M},\Phi) meets the above criteria, then the resulting behaviour possesses all hallmarks of intelligent reasoning (coherence, distributed consensus, conceptual breakthroughs) without any agent, planner, or symbolic module.

Proof. The gradient flow is autonomous; the soft sheaf mechanism is embedded in \Phi; Morse transitions emerge from the geometry. ∎

The definition is falsifiable: one can test whether a candidate cognitive system’s trajectory converges to a minimum, whether its distributed version achieves soft consensus, and whether its energy landscape is Morse. This cleanly separates the mathematical model from its empirical validation.

