Appendix C: Convergence to a Single Critical Point via the Kurdyka–Łojasiewicz Inequality

We present a rigorous proof that every bounded gradient trajectory of a real‑analytic proper potential converges to a single critical point, under the mild additional assumption that critical points are isolated. This proof corrects the gap noted in the critical review by providing a full capture lemma and length estimate.

C.1 The Kurdyka–Łojasiewicz Inequality

Definition C.1 (KL property). A differentiable function \Phi:\mathcal{M}\to\mathbb{R} satisfies the Kurdyka–Łojasiewicz inequality at a point x^* if there exist a neighbourhood U of x^*, constants c>0 and \theta\in[0,1), and a strictly increasing, concave function \varphi:[0,\eta)\to[0,\infty) with \varphi(0)=0, \varphi\in C^1(0,\eta), such that for all x\in U with \Phi(x^*)<\Phi(x)<\Phi(x^*)+\eta,

\varphi'\bigl(\Phi(x)-\Phi(x^*)\bigr)\,\|\nabla_g\Phi(x)\|_g \ge 1.
\]  

When \varphi(s) = c\,s^{1-\theta}, the inequality becomes \|\nabla_g\Phi(x)\|_g \ge c'(\Phi(x)-\Phi(x^*))^\theta.

Fact C.2. Every real‑analytic function on a real‑analytic manifold satisfies the KL inequality at every point (Łojasiewicz, 1963). If the point is a critical point, one may take \theta\in(0,1/2]; the constants and neighbourhood may depend on the point.

C.2 Capture Lemma

Lemma C.3 (Capture near an isolated critical point).
Let \Phi be C^2, proper, and bounded below, and suppose all critical points of \Phi are isolated. Let x(t) be a bounded gradient trajectory, and let x^* be a point in its \omega-limit set \omega(x_0). Then x^* is a critical point, and there exists a neighbourhood V of x^* and a time t_0 such that x(t)\in V for all t\ge t_0. Moreover, \Phi(x(t)) > \Phi(x^*) for all large t unless the trajectory hits x^* in finite time (which can only happen if the initial condition is x^*).

Proof. The \omega-limit set of a bounded gradient trajectory is non‑empty, compact, connected, and consists entirely of critical points (see, e.g., Absil et al., 2008). Since critical points are isolated, \omega(x_0) must be a single point; let \omega(x_0)=\{x^*\}. Thus x(t_n)\to x^* for some sequence t_n\to\infty.

Because x^* is isolated, we can choose a compact neighbourhood K of x^* that contains no other critical points. Let U\subset K be a smaller open neighbourhood whose closure is contained in the interior of K.
Assume, for contradiction, that the trajectory leaves U infinitely often. Then there exists a sequence \tau_k\to\infty with x(\tau_k)\notin U. By compactness of the sublevel set \{ \Phi\le \Phi(x_0)\}, the sequence x(\tau_k) has a convergent subsequence (still denoted \tau_k) converging to some y with \Phi(y)=\lim_k\Phi(x(\tau_k)) = \Phi(x^*) (because \Phi(x(t)) decreases to \Phi(x^*)). Since y is in the closure of the sublevel set and \Phi(y)=\Phi(x^*), it is a critical point. But y\notin U by construction, and y\neq x^* because U contains x^*. This contradicts the isolation of x^* as the unique critical point at energy \Phi(x^*). Therefore, there exists t_0 such that x(t)\in U for all t\ge t_0. ∎

C.3 Finite Length and Convergence

Theorem C.4 (Convergence under isolation and KL).
Let \Phi be C^2, proper, bounded below, and real‑analytic on a complete Riemannian manifold \mathcal{M}. Assume all critical points of \Phi are isolated. Then every bounded gradient trajectory x(t) converges to a unique critical point x^* as t\to\infty, and the trajectory has finite length:

\int_0^\infty \|\dot{x}(t)\|_g\,dt < \infty.

Proof. By Lemma C.3, the omega‑limit set is \{x^*\} and there exists a neighbourhood U and t_0 such that x(t)\in U for t\ge t_0. Since \Phi is real‑analytic, it satisfies the KL inequality at x^*. Hence we can choose U small enough so that the KL inequality holds on U: there exist c>0, \theta\in[0,1), such that

\|\nabla_g\Phi(x)\|_g \ge c\,(\Phi(x)-\Phi(x^*))^\theta \quad\text{whenever } x\in U,\;\Phi(x)>\Phi(x^*).
\]  

(For \theta=1 the inequality would give exponential decay, but the standard KL constant satisfies \theta\in[0,1).)
Define h(t)=\Phi(x(t))-\Phi(x^*)\ge 0, which is non‑increasing and strictly positive as long as x(t)\neq x^*. For t\ge t_0, the trajectory stays in U and

\frac{d}{dt}h(t) = -\|\nabla_g\Phi(x(t))\|_g^2 \le -c^2 h(t)^{2\theta}.
\]  

If \theta=1/2, this is h' \le -c^2 h, implying exponential decay of h. In any case, a standard integration yields finite length. More elegantly, we use the KL function \varphi(s)=c' s^{1-\theta}. For t\ge t_0,

\frac{d}{dt}\varphi(h(t)) = \varphi'(h(t))\,h'(t) = c'(1-\theta)h(t)^{-\theta}(-\|\nabla_g\Phi\|^2) \le -c'(1-\theta)c\,h(t)^{\theta}\|\nabla_g\Phi\| = -\tilde{c}\|\nabla_g\Phi\|,
\]  

where we used \|\nabla_g\Phi\|\ge c h^\theta. Thus

\frac{d}{dt}\varphi(h(t)) \le -\tilde{c}\,\|\dot{x}(t)\|_g.
\]  

Integrating from t_0 to T gives

\varphi(h(T)) - \varphi(h(t_0)) \le -\tilde{c}\int_{t_0}^T \|\dot{x}(t)\|_g\,dt \le 0.
\]  

Hence \int_{t_0}^\infty \|\dot{x}(t)\|_g\,dt \le \frac{1}{\tilde{c}}\varphi(h(t_0)) < \infty. Finite length implies that x(t) is Cauchy in the complete metric space (\mathcal{M},d_g); therefore x(t) converges to a unique limit, which must be x^*. ∎

Remark C.5. The isolation assumption is satisfied by Morse functions, which are generic. For fully general real‑analytic functions (allowing non‑isolated critical points), a deeper theorem of Kurdyka (1998) shows that every bounded gradient trajectory of a subanalytic function on a Riemannian manifold has finite length and converges to a critical point, without isolation. We may therefore drop the isolation hypothesis by invoking that result; the proof is beyond the scope of these appendices but the statement is referenced appropriately.

