Appendix D: Soft Sheaf Construction — Global Potential from Distributed Modules

We now give a rigorous mathematical construction that realises distributed cognitive modules as a single gradient flow on a product manifold, with a consistency‑penalty potential. This serves as a proof of Theorem 7.1.

Setting. For i=1,\dots,m, let (\mathcal{M}_i, g_i) be a complete Riemannian manifold and \Phi_i:\mathcal{M}_i\to\mathbb{R} be a C^2 proper, real‑analytic, bounded below function. Additionally, for each pair (i,j) we are given a divergence function D_{ij}:\mathcal{M}_i\times\mathcal{M}_j\to[0,\infty) that is smooth, symmetric in the sense D_{ij}(x_i,x_j)=D_{ji}(x_j,x_i), and such that D_{ij}(x_i,x_j)=0 iff x_i and x_j are “consistent” (i.e., belong to a prescribed diagonal relation). We require that D_{ij} is real‑analytic and that for any fixed x_j, the function x_i\mapsto D_{ij}(x_i,x_j) is proper.

Global manifold and metric. Define \mathcal{M} = \mathcal{M}_1\times \cdots \times\mathcal{M}_m with the product Riemannian metric g = g_1\oplus\cdots\oplus g_m. Completeness of each factor implies \mathcal{M} is complete.

Potential. Choose a constant \lambda>0 and define \Phi:\mathcal{M}\to\mathbb{R} by

\Phi(x_1,\dots,x_m) = \sum_{i=1}^m \Phi_i(x_i) + \lambda \sum_{1\le i<j\le m} D_{ij}(x_i,x_j).
\]  

All summands are real‑analytic, so \Phi is real‑analytic. Because each \Phi_i is proper and the D_{ij} are proper in each argument, the sublevel sets of \Phi are compact. Indeed, if \Phi(x)\le C, then each \Phi_i(x_i)\le C and D_{ij}(x_i,x_j)\le C/\lambda, so each x_i lies in a compact set. Hence \Phi is proper and bounded below.

Gradient flow. By Theorem A.1, the gradient flow on \mathcal{M} with potential \Phi exists globally and is unique. The flow decomposes as

\dot{x}_i = -\nabla_{g_i}\Phi_i(x_i) - \lambda \sum_{j\neq i} \nabla_{x_i} D_{ij}(x_i,x_j),
\]  

where the gradient of D_{ij} with respect to the first argument is taken.

Convergence. Since \Phi is real‑analytic, proper, and bounded below, and critical points are generically isolated (or we may invoke the general subanalytic convergence theorem), Theorem C.4 ensures that every bounded trajectory converges to a critical point of \Phi. A local minimum of \Phi is a point where the local potentials are minimised and the inter‑module divergences are as small as possible—an optimal soft global section.

Thus the distributed system, without any central coordinator, spontaneously evolves toward a state of maximal coherence.

Remark D.1. The specific choice of divergence D_{ij} and the constant \lambda tunes the trade‑off between local fidelity and global consistency. The framework can accommodate any smooth divergence; the KL divergence used in the main text is a particular example.

