Appendix E: Morse Theory, Persistent Homology, and Cognitive Phase Transitions

This appendix formalises the link between gradient flow on a Morse cognitive potential and topological changes in the sublevel sets.

E.1 Morse Functions and Gradient Flow

Definition E.1. A smooth function \Phi:\mathcal{M}\to\mathbb{R} is a Morse function if all its critical points are non‑degenerate (Hessian is non‑singular).

Morse functions are generic (in the C^2 topology). For a Morse function, critical points are isolated and have a well‑defined Morse index.

Fact E.2 (Morse Lemma). Around any critical point x^* of index k, there exist local coordinates such that

\Phi(x) = \Phi(x^*) - x_1^2 - \cdots - x_k^2 + x_{k+1}^2 + \cdots + x_n^2.

E.2 Sublevel Filtration and Cell Attachment

Define the sublevel set \mathcal{M}_a = \{x\in\mathcal{M} : \Phi(x)\le a\}. If a<b and the interval [a,b] contains no critical values of \Phi, then \mathcal{M}_a and \mathcal{M}_b are diffeomorphic. When a crosses a critical value c=\Phi(x^*) with index k, the topology of the sublevel set changes by the attachment of a k-cell.

Theorem E.3 (Morse Theory). Let \Phi be a proper Morse function on a complete manifold. Then for any critical value c with a single critical point of index k, the set \mathcal{M}_{c+\varepsilon} is homotopy equivalent to \mathcal{M}_{c-\varepsilon} with a k-cell attached.

Proof. This is a standard result; see Milnor, Morse Theory, Lemma 3.1. ∎

E.3 Gradient Flow and Crossing Critical Values

Consider a gradient trajectory x(t) with initial energy \Phi(x_0). As t increases, the energy strictly decreases except when the trajectory is at a critical point. Since the trajectory converges to a critical point x^* (Theorem C.4), the energy \Phi(x(t)) sweeps through a range of values. At the moment the energy drops below a critical value c that is higher than \Phi(x^*), the sublevel set \mathcal{M}_{\Phi(x(t))} undergoes a topological change.

Theorem E.4 (Cognitive Phase Transitions). Let \Phi be a proper Morse function and x(t) a bounded gradient trajectory converging to a local minimum x^*. Suppose the energy of x^* is c_{\min} and the trajectory initially has energy c_0. Let c_1>c_2>\cdots>c_{\min} be the critical values crossed by the trajectory. At each crossing time t_k when \Phi(x(t_k))=c_k, the topology of the set of states of coherence level at most \Phi(x(t_k)) changes by the attachment of cells according to the Morse index(es) of the critical point(s) at level c_k. A paradigmatic “insight” corresponds to the death of a connected component (0‑dimensional feature) or the vanishing of a circular contradiction (1‑dimensional hole), etc.

Proof. Since the trajectory is strictly decreasing except at isolated critical points, it crosses each critical value at a unique time (unless it passes through multiple critical points of the same value, which is non‑generic). The Morse theorem applies locally. ∎

E.4 Persistent Homology Stability

The persistent homology of the filtration \{\mathcal{M}_\varepsilon\} is a topological signature of the cognitive landscape. The stability theorem (Cohen-Steiner et al., 2007) guarantees that small perturbations of \Phi yield persistence diagrams with bounded bottleneck distance. Thus the topological features (paradigm shifts) are robust.

