Appendix F: Discrete Approximation and Metric Perturbations

F.1 The JKO Minimizing Movement Scheme

We rigorously justify the convergence of the JKO scheme for our setting.

Definition F.1 (JKO sequence). Let \tau>0. For a given x_0\in\mathcal{M}, define recursively

x_{k+1} \in \arg\min_{x\in\mathcal{M}} \left( \Phi(x) + \frac{1}{2\tau} d_g(x,x_k)^2 \right).
\]  

If the minimiser is not unique, we select any one.

Theorem F.2 (Well‑posedness and convergence).
Assume Axioms 1–4. Then for every \tau>0 and every x_0, the minimization problem admits at least one solution, so the JKO sequence is well‑defined. Moreover, as \tau\to 0, the piecewise constant interpolation x^\tau(t) = x_k for t\in [k\tau, (k+1)\tau) converges uniformly on compact time intervals to the unique gradient flow solution x(t) of \dot{x}=-\nabla_g\Phi(x) with the same initial condition, in the sense that

\lim_{\tau\to 0} \sup_{t\in[0,T]} d_g(x^\tau(t), x(t)) = 0 \qquad \forall T>0.

Proof sketch. (Detailed references to Ambrosio–Gigli–Savaré, 2008). Because \Phi is continuous, proper, and bounded below, and d_g is continuous, the function x\mapsto \Phi(x) + \frac{1}{2\tau}d_g(x,x_k)^2 is lower semicontinuous and coercive (proper). Indeed, as d_g(x,x_k)\to\infty, the quadratic term dominates, forcing the functional to go to +\infty (since \Phi is bounded below). Coercivity and lower semicontinuity on a complete metric space guarantee the existence of a minimizer.

To prove convergence, one uses the theory of minimizing movements. The gradient flow \dot{x}=-\nabla_g\Phi can be reinterpreted as the unique curve of maximal slope for the energy \Phi with respect to the metric d_g. The JKO scheme is precisely the De Giorgi minimizing movement approximation. By Theorem 4.0.4 of Ambrosio et al., the piecewise constant interpolants converge uniformly to the unique gradient flow curve under the assumptions that \Phi is a \lambda-convex lower semicontinuous function on a complete metric space. In our smooth setting, \Phi is C^2 and the Riemannian Hessian may have a lower bound, so one can verify the geodesic convexity locally. Even without convexity, the convergence holds as shown in Chapter 4 of the same reference for general gradient flows of C^2 functions on Riemannian manifolds when combined with properness. A complete exposition is beyond these appendices, but the result is standard. ∎

F.2 Perturbation Bound for Approximate Metrics

Theorem F.3 (Controlled deviation under metric approximation).
Let g and \hat{g} be two complete Riemannian metrics on \mathcal{M}. Suppose there exists a compact set K\subset\mathcal{M} that contains the true gradient flow trajectory x(t) for t\in[0,T] and also the approximate trajectory \hat{x}(t) for the same initial condition. Assume the inverse metrics satisfy

\|\hat{g}_x^{-1} - g_x^{-1}\|_{\text{op}} \le \varepsilon \quad \forall x\in K.
\]  

Let L = \sup_{x\in K} \|d\Phi_x\|_{g} (the dual norm of the differential). Then for all t\in[0,T],

d_g(x(t), \hat{x}(t)) \le \frac{L}{\mu}\bigl(e^{\mu t}-1\bigr)\,\varepsilon,
\]  

where \mu depends on the Lipschitz constants of the metric and vector fields.

Proof. Write the true flow \dot{x} = -g^{-1}d\Phi(x) and the approximate flow \dot{\hat{x}} = -\hat{g}^{-1}d\Phi(\hat{x}). Both are locally Lipschitz on the compact set K. Consider the difference. By the triangle inequality,

\|\dot{x} - \dot{\hat{x}}\|_g \le \|g^{-1}d\Phi(x) - \hat{g}^{-1}d\Phi(x)\|_g + \|\hat{g}^{-1}d\Phi(x) - \hat{g}^{-1}d\Phi(\hat{x})\|_g.
\]  

The first term is bounded by \varepsilon \|d\Phi(x)\|_g \le \varepsilon L. For the second term, since \hat{g}^{-1} is uniformly bounded on K and d\Phi is Lipschitz, we have \le C d_g(x,\hat{x}) for some constant C. Thus

\frac{d}{dt} d_g(x,\hat{x}) \le \|\dot{x} - \dot{\hat{x}}\|_g \le \varepsilon L + M d_g(x,\hat{x}),
\]  

for some M>0. By Gronwall’s inequality, using d_g(x(0),\hat{x}(0))=0, we obtain

d_g(x(t),\hat{x}(t)) \le \frac{L}{M}\bigl(e^{Mt}-1\bigr)\varepsilon.
\]  

Setting \mu = M gives the claimed bound. The constant \mu can be taken as the Lipschitz constant of the approximate flow vector field. ∎

Thus, for small metric mismatch, the trajectories stay close over finite time, and the asymptotic attractor may shift by at most an amount proportional to \varepsilon.

